From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: glink: take a reference for the rcid entry too

The driver's invariant is that every idr entry owns one reference on the
channel. Both close paths spell it out:

  qcom_glink_rx_close()      idr_remove(&glink->rcids, ...) then kref_put()
  qcom_glink_rx_close_ack()  idr_remove(&glink->lcids, ...) then kref_put()

and qcom_glink_native_remove() does the same in bulk, one put per lcids entry
and one per rcids entry.

qcom_glink_rx_open() breaks that invariant in exactly one case. It takes a
reference for the rcids entry only when it allocated the channel itself, which
its own error path states outright:

	free_channel:
		/* Release the reference, iff we took it */
		if (create_device)
			kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);

Writing the accounting out for both directions, with kref_init() giving 1:

  remote-initiated (the far end sends OPEN first)
    qcom_glink_rx_open()      alloc_channel      1   <- owns the rcids entry
    qcom_glink_create_remote() -> send_open_req  2   <- owns the lcids entry
    teardown                                    -2   -> 0, balanced

  locally-initiated (rpmsg_create_ept, i.e. rpmsg_ctrl's
                     RPMSG_CREATE_EPT_IOCTL from userspace)
    qcom_glink_create_local() alloc_channel      1   <- owns the endpoint
                              send_open_req      2   <- owns the lcids entry
    qcom_glink_rx_open()      idr_alloc(rcids)   2   <- owns NOTHING
    teardown                                    -2   -> 0 with the endpoint
                                                       still holding a pointer

So a channel opened from this side and then acknowledged by the remote sits in
both idrs with one reference too few. The rcids put steals the endpoint's
reference. If the endpoint has already been released the count is 1 rather
than 2 when the two puts land, and the second one underflows:

	refcount_t: underflow; use-after-free.
	WARNING: lib/refcount.c:28 at refcount_warn_saturate+0xd8/0x13c,
	         CPU#6: kworker/u32:5/128
	Workqueue: rproc_recovery_wq rproc_crash_handler_work
	Call trace:
	 refcount_warn_saturate+0xd8/0x13c (P)
	 qcom_glink_native_remove+0x160/0x268
	 qcom_glink_smem_unregister+0x28/0x934 [qcom_glink_smem]
	 glink_subdev_stop+0x1c/0x30 [qcom_common]
	 rproc_stop+0xb4/0x144
	 rproc_boot_recovery+0x30/0xc4
	 rproc_trigger_recovery+0x70/0x84
	 rproc_crash_handler_work+0xa8/0xe0

A second, independent signature of the same missing reference was then caught
on the same machine, and it is the more convincing of the two because no modem
crash is involved at all. Within one boot, using rpmsg_char's RPMSG_CREATE_EPT
on the modem's DS channel:

	[  191.909585] close ack on unknown channel
	[  191.909913] WARNING: drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c:1755
	                        at qcom_glink_work+0x46c/0x714
	               x23: dead000000000100
	               Workqueue: events qcom_glink_work

	[  885.474811] rpmsg rpmsg0: failed to open DS

	[  925.532523] Unable to handle kernel paging request at
	                        virtual address 0000000000010000
	[  925.534463] Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000006 [#1] SMP
	               pc : __pi_strcmp+0x24/0x140
	               lr : qcom_glink_create_ept+0x74/0x2dc
	               x0 : 0000000000010000   x3 : 0000000000005344
	Call trace:
	 __pi_strcmp+0x24/0x140 (P)
	 rpmsg_create_ept+0x48/0x78
	 rpmsg_eptdev_open+0x9c/0x124 [rpmsg_char]
	 chrdev_open+0x16c/0x200
	 do_dentry_open / vfs_open / path_openat / do_sys_openat2
	 __arm64_sys_openat+0x70/0x9c

which reads straight through. The over-put frees the channel early, so the
close ack that follows no longer finds it in lcids and warns with list poison
still in the registers. Nothing removes the dangling pointer from rcids, so it
stays reachable. qcom_glink_create_ept() then walks rcids comparing names, and
strcmp() dereferences the freed channel's name: x3 holds 0x5344, the "DS" it
was looking for, and x0 is what is left of the other side. An unprivileged
sequence of open() calls on a character device should not be able to do that.

Observed on a Motorola Moto G82 5G (SM6375) after userspace had opened and
then released an endpoint on the modem's DS channel and the modem
subsequently crashed. qcom_glink_rx_close() takes the same over-put on an
ordinary remote close of a locally-opened channel, so the teardown path is
where it was seen rather than the only way to reach it.

Take the reference when the rcids entry is created rather than only when the
channel is allocated, and release it unconditionally on the error path, which
is right for both cases: in the create_device case it is the allocation
reference being dropped, otherwise it is the one just taken.

--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
@@ -1652,6 +1652,13 @@ static int qcom_glink_rx_open(struct qcom_glink *glink, unsigned int rcid,
 
 		/* The opening dance was initiated by the remote */
 		create_device = true;
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * The channel is already in lcids and holds a reference for
+		 * that entry. qcom_glink_native_remove() puts one reference
+		 * per idr, so the rcids entry needs one of its own.
+		 */
+		kref_get(&channel->refcount);
 	}
 
 	trace_qcom_glink_cmd_open_rx(glink->label, name, channel->lcid, rcid);
@@ -1706,9 +1713,8 @@ rcid_remove:
 	channel->rcid = 0;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&glink->idr_lock, flags);
 free_channel:
-	/* Release the reference, iff we took it */
-	if (create_device)
-		kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);
+	/* Release the rcids reference, whichever of the two it was */
+	kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);
 
 	return ret;
 }
